        }
      ],
      "answer": "To understand the difference, it helps to look at how both approaches handle parameter-efficient fine-tuning (PEFT):\n\n* **LoRA (Low-Rank Adaptation)** freezes the pretrained model weights and represents weight updates using the product of two small, low-rank matrices. This drastically reduces the number of trainable parameters while keeping the base model in standard precision (e.g., 16-bit).\n* **QLoRA (Quantized LoRA)** builds upon LoRA by quantizing the base model weights down to a lower precision (such as 4-bit). It then applies LoRA on top of these quantized weights, which significantly reduces memory consumption and allows much larger models to be fine-tuned on smaller GPUs.\n\nThink about how quantization affects the memory footprint of the base weights during training versus leaving them unquantized. How might that trade-off impact your choice of hardware?\n\nLecture 02",
